well it's a great pleasure to be able to  join in this tribute to clyde  who became a good friend as well as a  good colleague over the years  he was a one-off nobody quite liked  clyde  his enthusiasm and energy and joy of eve  will be  missed by all of us  and in a very real sense he injected  productive energy  into the cbd program  he was a very good scientist  but he was working with  a scintillating collection  of amazing  international stars  what he had particularly was a strategic  vision to pull the group together  now to be easy to assume  that inevitably the program will be the  weaker  as a result of his absence  and of course we will miss the fun and  excitement that he provided but to focus  on what we will miss  is to entirely overlook  the tremendous contributions he made  that will continue to live on  and indeed continue to grow because they  have now such a firm basis  what i would like to do  is to mention briefly what i see as his  major contributions  before going on to note the challenges  that remain  i label these challenges  rather than unanswered questions  because clyde would never have been  dismayed  by the extent to which so much remains  unknown  indeed  instead as always with his  insatiable curiosity he would be looking  for ways to meet those challenges  his lasting legacy should be the  determination  given to us to solve the problems  provided by those challenges  that is a cbd  determination  that will i'm sure bring rich rewards  his achievements well the first i think  was to use clyde's phrase the  recognition of the importance of the  biological embedding of experiences  he was not the first to identify the  phenomenon that goes back over half a  century but he was one of the first to  put it at the very top of the agenda  at a time when so many psychosocial  researchers remained adamantly  uninterested in biology  for clyde following on biologists such  as robert hind  to regard biology and the environment as  entirely separate was a complete  nonsense  second he emphasized the crucial  importance of identifying the mechanisms  that mediated the environmental impact  on the biology  it would be only a slight exaggeration  to say that he had a conversion from a  geographical  epidemiologist  to an epigenetic  evangelist epigenetics constituting as  we have heard the chemical changes that  influence gene expression  but to describe clyde's involvement in  epigenetics as a conversion  is nevertheless an exaggeration  because clyde appreciated that  epigenetics  constituted just one of many  possible biological mediators of  environmental effects  meaning the mechanisms involved in their  effects  everyone's genes are as we have heard  fixed from the outset  but their functional effects  only  come about when the genes are expressed  in the tissues  epigenetics concerns the features  outside of the genes themselves  that shape the influence of the genes  the tasks are to delineate the several  interacting processes involved and clyde  would have played a key role in meeting  that challenge  third  building on his background as an  epidemiologist  he emphasized the narrowness of an  exclusive focus on individual  causes only at an individual level  why was it  that some  population groups had rates of crime  family breakdown poverty and  unemployment so much higher than others  living in nearby areas  was this just because birds of a feather  flocked together or were the social  forces of some kind  at work  what was striking too  that clyde was insistent that such  population approaches had to include  health and illness indices  that implied of course that the  solutions too might be best applied to  populations  and not just to high-risk individuals  finally although not himself primarily a  trialist or evaluator of interventions  he was certainly not an ivory tower  academic  for him it mattered greatly that  knowledge be put to good purposes  whether in terms of policy prevention or  intervention once more a key cfar goal  so  what are the challenges to be faced  first if all environments tend to have  epigenetic effects  or hpa effects or structural brain  changes  is there a degree of mildness below  which there are few if any epigenetic  changes  does the type of environment matter  are acute stress is the same as chronic  adversities the evidence certainly  indicates that the hpaf hpa effects are  not the same for the two but does that  also apply to the epigenetics  do pleasurable positive experiences  have the same biological effects as  unpleasant abusive ones  can the pattern of epigenetic changes  differentiate abuse from neglect  and both of those from social  disadvantage  can the biological changes differentiate  between  sensitivity and stealing or  strengthening effects  we know that some challenges enable  individuals  to come through stronger and less  vulnerable than before  equally however  in other individuals or other  circumstances  they become sensitized so that they  exhibit  increased susceptibility to later  stresses  is this difference reflected  in a different biology  most fundamentally do we have sensitive  and discriminating measures of the  relevant environmental features  can the biology be informative  on the likely health effects  it's all very well  to identify the stress physiology but  what connection does this have  with either physical or mental ill  health  one set  of key features that differentiates  responses  to the environment are the polymorphic  genetic markers that is the genetic  features that determine individual  differences  but what are the mediating biological  processes  do they as some suggested involve  epigenetic changes  also  do they deal just with responses to  adverse environments or rather do they  involve responsivity to both good and  bad environments  it seems evolutionary impossible  that there would be genes concerned only  with damaging environments  perhaps the mechanism involved is a  general responsivity to all environments  if that proved to be the case there  would be important implications for  treatment and prevention  there are other challenges but there's  time to mention only one  more in the domain of biomarkers it is  common to find biological markers or  indices  that differentiate groups  in terms of health outcomes  but it's rare indeed to find markers  that are sufficiently reliable and  sufficiently valid  to be of use for differentiating  individuals who will or will not develop  disorders  can those be developed to a point when  they are useful at an individual level  well  it would have been great of course if  clyde was still here to help  with answering these questions because  undoubtedly he would  and  his creative imagination  and looking forward would have been a  help  much remains to be done but helped by  clyde's vision  energy and collaborative endeavors his  legacy will give cbd  the strength and creative ideas to  achieve what he was aiming for  you
 

